index feb36d7..e5e3155 100644
--- a/docs/users-policy.md
+++ b/docs/users-policy.md
@@ -43,7 +43,7 @@ Each entry:
 
 A principal with **no `policy` field** (or the legacy `user`/`password` pair) has full access — this matches S2's pre-multi-user behavior, so existing single-credential configs keep working unchanged. A principal with a `policy` that has **no matching statement** for a given action is denied by default (deny-by-default once a policy is attached).
 
-**Policy changes require a server restart.** Config (including `users` and every `policy`) is loaded once at startup; there is no hot-reload or `SIGHUP` handling. Editing the config file or environment variables while the server is running has no effect until you restart it.
+**Credential and policy changes can be applied with `SIGHUP`.** Send `SIGHUP` to the running process (`kill -HUP <pid>`) after editing the config file: the server rebuilds a candidate configuration with the same precedence as startup (defaults &lt; config file &lt; environment variables &lt; explicit command-line flags), validates it as a whole, and atomically publishes it as a new configuration generation. Only the legacy `user`/`password` pair and the `users` list (each entry's credentials and `policy`) may change at runtime; a candidate that also changes the listen addresses, storage type/root, initial buckets, health path, upload limits, or any other runtime/resource field is rejected in full, and the previous generation keeps serving. An unreadable or missing file, malformed JSON, an invalid policy, or a duplicate `access_key_id` likewise leaves the previous generation untouched (the outcome is logged distinctly); fix the source and send `SIGHUP` again to recover. Each request authenticates against one complete snapshot taken at request start, so in-flight requests finish against the old generation while new requests use the new one. Deployments started without a config file ignore `SIGHUP`.
 
 ## Policy grammar
 
